/** \addtogroup area_complexity */
/** \defgroup theory_complexity Theory of complexity
 \ingroup area_complexity

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/complexity` realize chapter 18 of the
 book and the polynomial cases of its conclusion, i.e., the problems of the
 class NP with their certificates and their enumerations, the simulation of
 a nondeterministic algorithm, the polynomial reductions among the problems,
 and the polynomial algorithms of a few problems close to them. This page
 collects what the headers take for granted: the definitions of the classes
 P and NP and the notation (see \ref theory_complexity_notation), the
 nondeterministic algorithms and the cost of their simulation (see
 \ref theory_complexity_nd), the size of the trees that the enumerations
 visit (see \ref theory_complexity_enum), the reductions and the chain of
 NP-complete problems that the library builds (see
 \ref theory_complexity_red), and the arguments behind the polynomial cases
 (see \ref theory_complexity_tiling). The pseudocode of each algorithm is
 instead on the page of its header, in the comment of its function, together
 with the section of the book, or the paper, that it follows; the strategies
 for the problems that are hard are the subject of the theory of intractable
 problems (`include/ADS/intractable`).

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_complexity_notation Definitions and notation

 A <em>decision problem</em> \f$ \Pi \f$ is a set of instances, each with the
 answer YES or NO, and \f$ Y_\Pi \f$ is the set of the instances whose answer
 is YES; the problems of optimization (the largest clique, the shortest tour)
 become decision problems by a bound \f$ k \f$ on the value (whether a
 clique of \f$ k \f$ nodes exists), which is as hard up to a polynomial factor,
 since a search on \f$ k \f$ finds the optimum with a logarithmic number of
 questions. The size \f$ |x| \f$ of an instance is the length of a
 reasonable encoding of it, with the numbers written in binary, so that a
 number \f$ k \f$ counts \f$ \Theta( \log k ) \f$ and not \f$ k \f$ (the
 distinction on which the pseudopolynomial algorithms rest). The class
 <em>P</em> holds the problems decided by an algorithm in time
 \f$ O( |x|^c ) \f$ for a constant \f$ c \f$; the class <em>NP</em> those
 that have a polynomial <em>certificate</em>, i.e., a polynomial \f$ p \f$
 and an algorithm \f$ V \f$ of polynomial time such that
 \f[
   x \in Y_\Pi \iff \exists\, y : \ |y| \le p( |x| ) \ \text{and}
   \ V( x , y ) = \text{YES} . \tag{1}
 \f]
 The functions `cliqueCertificate()`, `tspCertificate()`, `satCertificate()`
 and `legalTiling()`, and the postconditions of the other enumerations of
 `Problems.h`, are such algorithms \f$ V \f$, each with a certificate of
 polynomial size (a set of nodes, a tour, an assignment, a covering); hence
 every problem of the area is in NP, and \f$ \mathrm{P} \subseteq
 \mathrm{NP} \f$, since a polynomial algorithm is a checker that ignores
 \f$ y \f$. Whether the inclusion is strict is open.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ x \f$, \f$ |x| \f$</td>
     <td>an instance and its size, the numbers in binary</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ y \f$, \f$ V( x , y ) \f$</td>
     <td>a certificate and its checker, of polynomial size and time</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ f \f$, \f$ A \propto B \f$</td>
     <td>a reduction, of polynomial time, with \f$ x \in Y_A \iff
         f( x ) \in Y_B \f$, and the relation it proves</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ G \f$, \f$ n \f$, \f$ m \f$</td>
     <td>a graph as an adjacency matrix (<tt>Adjacency</tt>), its nodes
         and its edges; its diagonal is ignored</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ F \f$, \f$ \pm( v + 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>a formula in conjunctive normal form (<tt>Cnf</tt>), and the
         literal of the variable \f$ v \f$ or of its negation</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ k \f$</td>
     <td>the bound of a decision problem: the size of a clique or of a
         cover, the length of a tour, the colors, the profit</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ D \f$, \f$ d = D[ 0 ] \f$, \f$ u_{hij} \f$</td>
     <td>the tiles of the tiling, the tile of the corner, and the variable
         or node of the tile \f$ h \f$ in the square
         \f$ ( i , j ) \f$ (<tt>tileVar()</tt>)</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_complexity_nd Nondeterminism

 A <em>nondeterministic</em> algorithm may call `choice( k )`, which returns
 one of \f$ k \f$ alternatives, and ends with success or failure; it answers
 YES on \f$ x \f$ if some sequence of choices leads to success, and its time
 is that of the longest sequence of choices (section 18.2). An algorithm of
 this kind that runs in polynomial time is a certificate in disguise: the
 sequence of the choices that leads to success is a \f$ y \f$ of (1), and
 conversely the algorithm that chooses the bits of \f$ y \f$ one by one and
 then runs \f$ V( x , y ) \f$ succeeds if and only if (1) holds. Hence NP is
 also the class of the problems decided in polynomial time by a
 nondeterministic algorithm, whence its name.

 <em>Lemma 1 (the simulation).</em> A nondeterministic algorithm that makes at
 most \f$ h \f$ choices of at most \f$ k \f$ alternatives each, in time
 \f$ t \f$, is simulated by a deterministic one in time
 \f[
   O( k^h \, t ) . \tag{2}
 \f]

 Indeed, the sequences of choices are the paths from the root of a tree of
 height at most \f$ h \f$ whose nodes have at most \f$ k \f$ children, and the
 tree has at most \f$ k^h \f$ leaves. `Chooser::run()` of `Nondeterminism.h`
 replays the algorithm once per leaf, in lexicographic order, each run
 costing \f$ O( t ) \f$: after a failure it drops the last choices that have
 no alternative left and advances the one before them, which is the visit in
 depth of the tree with a backtrack on the last choice still open (section
 18.3). With \f$ h \f$ and \f$ t \f$ polynomial in \f$ |x| \f$, (2) is
 \f$ 2^{O( |x|^c )} \f$; hence \f$ \mathrm{NP} \subseteq \mathrm{EXP} \f$, and
 no simulation essentially faster than the visit of the tree is known for an
 NP-complete problem.

 \section theory_complexity_enum Certificates and enumerations

 The enumerations of `Problems.h` and `squareTiling()` of `Domino.h` are (2)
 specialized to each problem: a backtrack builds the certificate one piece
 at a time and checks each partial certificate, so that a subtree whose root
 already fails the check is skipped. The tree of the choices is that of the
 subsets, of the permutations or of the functions of the problem, and the
 table gives its size, i.e., the time in the worst case up to the
 polynomial cost of a node.

 <table>
 <caption>The enumerations and the trees of their choices</caption>
 <tr><th>Problem</th><th>Function</th><th>Certificate</th>
     <th>Leaves of the tree</th></tr>
 <tr><td>CLIQUE, INDEPENDENT SET</td>
     <td><tt>clique()</tt>, <tt>independentSet()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ k \f$ nodes</td><td>\f$ \binom{n}{k} \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>VERTEX COVER</td><td><tt>vertexCover()</tt></td>
     <td>at most \f$ k \f$ nodes</td>
     <td>\f$ \sum_{s \le k} \binom{n}{s} \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>GRAPH COLORABILITY</td><td><tt>coloring()</tt></td>
     <td>a color per node</td><td>\f$ k^n \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>TRAVELING SALESMAN, the Hamiltonian problems</td>
     <td><tt>tsp()</tt>, <tt>hamiltonianCircuit()</tt>, ...</td>
     <td>a permutation</td><td>\f$ ( n - 1 )! \f$ or \f$ n! \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>SATISFIABILITY, 0-1 INTEGER PROGRAMMING</td>
     <td><tt>satisfiable()</tt>, <tt>lp01()</tt></td>
     <td>a 0-1 vector</td><td>\f$ 2^n \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>SUBSET SUM, PARTITION, KNAPSACK</td>
     <td><tt>subsetSumExists()</tt>, <tt>partition()</tt>,
         <tt>knapsackDecision()</tt></td>
     <td>a subset</td><td>\f$ 2^n \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>MULTIPROCESSOR SCHEDULING</td>
     <td><tt>multiprocessorScheduling()</tt></td>
     <td>a processor per program</td><td>\f$ m^n \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>EXACT COVER BY 3-SETS, 3-DIMENSIONAL MATCHING</td>
     <td><tt>exactCover3()</tt>, <tt>threeDimensionalMatching()</tt></td>
     <td>a subset of the sets</td><td>\f$ 2^{|Y|} \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>BOUNDED TILING</td><td><tt>squareTiling()</tt></td>
     <td>a tile per square</td><td>\f$ m^{n^2 - 1} \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 The pruning of the code changes the time in practice, never the bound: a
 partial tour longer than \f$ k \f$, a partial volume above the capacity or a
 sum above the target is not extended, and the symmetries are broken by
 fixing the city 0 at the start of the tours, the node 0 in the first half
 of a bisection, the colors in the order of their first use, and by trying a
 program on one empty processor only. A clause of `satisfiable()` is checked
 as soon as its largest variable is set, which cuts the tree at the first
 clause made false. Note that the same problems may have polynomial cases:
 the longest path of an acyclic graph (`longestPathDag()`) is computed by
 one scan of the nodes in topological order, since a path never comes back
 to a node already scanned, in \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$ on the matrix, while on a
 graph with cycles the longest simple path is NP-hard (see
 \ref theory_complexity_red).

 \section theory_complexity_red Reductions and NP-completeness

 A <em>polynomial reduction</em> of \f$ A \f$ to \f$ B \f$ is a function
 \f$ f \f$, computable in polynomial time, such that
 \f[
   x \in Y_A \iff f( x ) \in Y_B , \tag{3}
 \f]
 written \f$ A \propto B \f$. Since \f$ |f( x )| \f$ is at most polynomial in
 \f$ |x| \f$ (an algorithm of polynomial time writes no more), a polynomial
 algorithm for \f$ B \f$ composed with \f$ f \f$ is a polynomial algorithm for
 \f$ A \f$, and two reductions compose into one; hence \f$ \propto \f$ is
 transitive, and \f$ A \propto B \f$ with \f$ B \in \mathrm{P} \f$ gives
 \f$ A \in \mathrm{P} \f$. A problem is <em>NP-hard</em> if every problem of
 NP reduces to it, and <em>NP-complete</em> if it is also in NP; one
 NP-complete problem in P would give \f$ \mathrm{P} = \mathrm{NP} \f$, and by
 transitivity a problem of NP to which a known NP-complete problem reduces is
 NP-complete in its turn.

 <em>Theorem 2 (Cook and Levin).</em> SATISFIABILITY is NP-complete.

 The proof writes, for a nondeterministic algorithm of polynomial time and an
 input \f$ x \f$, a formula of polynomial size whose variables describe the
 contents of the memory at each step and whose clauses force each step to
 follow from the one before it; the formula is satisfiable if and only if a
 sequence of choices leads to success. The book starts the chain instead from
 BOUNDED TILING, whose rows of tiles can encode the successive
 configurations of a computation in the same way (the tiles of Wang), and
 reduces it to the other problems. The reductions of `Reductions.h` and of
 the functions that delegate to one another realize the chain below, each in
 the time needed to write its output.

 <table>
 <caption>The reductions realized (sections 18.5 and 18.7 of the
          book)</caption>
 <tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Where</th><th>Function</th></tr>
 <tr><td>BOUNDED TILING</td><td>CLIQUE</td><td>Theorem 18.2</td>
     <td><tt>dominoToClique()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>BOUNDED TILING</td><td>SATISFIABILITY</td><td>Theorem 18.3</td>
     <td><tt>dominoToSat()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>BOUNDED TILING</td><td>0-1 INTEGER PROGRAMMING</td>
     <td>Theorem 18.4</td><td><tt>dominoToLp01()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>CLIQUE</td><td>INDEPENDENT SET</td><td>Theorem 18.5</td>
     <td><tt>complement()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>INDEPENDENT SET</td><td>VERTEX COVER</td><td>exercise 18.11</td>
     <td>the complement of the set, \f$ k' = n - k \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>HAMILTONIAN CIRCUIT</td><td>TRAVELING SALESMAN</td>
     <td>Theorem 18.6</td><td><tt>hamiltonToTsp()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>PARTITION</td><td>MULTIPROCESSOR SCHEDULING, SUBSET SUM,
     KNAPSACK</td><td>Theorems 18.7 to 18.9</td>
     <td><tt>halfSum()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>DIRECTED HAMILTONIAN CIRCUIT</td>
     <td>DIRECTED HAMILTONIAN PATH</td><td>exercise 18.13</td>
     <td><tt>cycleToPath()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>DIRECTED HAMILTONIAN CIRCUIT</td><td>HAMILTONIAN PATH</td>
     <td>exercise 18.14</td><td><tt>cycleToChain()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>DIRECTED HAMILTONIAN PATH</td><td>LONGEST PATH</td>
     <td>exercise 18.15</td><td>\f$ k = n - 1 \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>HAMILTONIAN PATH</td><td>DEGREE-CONSTRAINED SPANNING TREE</td>
     <td>exercise 18.16</td><td>\f$ k = 2 \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>3-DIMENSIONAL MATCHING</td><td>EXACT COVER BY 3-SETS</td>
     <td>chapter 18</td><td><tt>threeDimensionalMatching()</tt></td></tr>
 </table>

 Each reduction rests on a correspondence between the certificates of the
 two problems. In `dominoToClique()` the nodes of one square are never
 joined, so that a clique of \f$ n^2 \f$ nodes takes exactly one tile per
 square, and two of its tiles in adjacent squares are joined only if they
 fit; hence the cliques of \f$ n^2 \f$ nodes are the legal coverings, and
 the graph has \f$ m n^2 \f$ nodes. In `complement()` a set is independent in
 \f$ G \f$ if and only if it is a clique of the complement, and \f$ S \f$ is
 independent if and only if \f$ V \setminus S \f$ covers the edges, since an
 edge left uncovered by \f$ V \setminus S \f$ has both ends in \f$ S \f$. In
 `hamiltonToTsp()` a tour has \f$ n \f$ edges of length at least 1, and its
 length is \f$ n \f$ if and only if all its edges are edges of \f$ G \f$. In
 `halfSum()` a set of numbers of sum \f$ s / 2 \f$ leaves the other half to
 the second processor, or to the objects left out of the knapsack, whose
 profits and volumes are the numbers themselves. The cost of each reduction
 is that of writing its output, e.g., \f$ \Theta( m^2 n^4 ) \f$ for the
 adjacency matrix of `dominoToClique()`.

 The same tool proves lower bounds within P. Sorting reduces to the convex
 hull (`sortToHull()`): the points \f$ ( a_i , a_i^2 ) \f$ lie on a parabola,
 all of them are vertices of the hull, and the hull lists them in the order
 of the \f$ a_i \f$; hence a hull in \f$ o( n \log n ) \f$ comparisons would
 sort in \f$ o( n \log n ) \f$, against the lower bound of the sorting by
 comparisons, and the convex hull costs \f$ \Omega( n \log n ) \f$ (section
 18.5).

 \section theory_complexity_tiling Polynomial cases

 The square tiling is NP-complete, while the tiling of a strip
 \f$ 1 \times n \f$ is in P (section 18.5): a strip is a walk of \f$ n - 1 \f$
 edges from \f$ d \f$ in the graph \f$ H \f$ whose nodes are the \f$ m \f$
 tiles and whose edges \f$ ( d_u , d_v ) \f$ join a tile to those that fit at
 its right.

 <em>Lemma 3 (the linear tiling).</em> A strip of \f$ n \f$ tiles from
 \f$ d \f$ exists if and only if
 \f[
   \text{a cycle of } H \text{ is reachable from } d
   \quad \text{or} \quad L( d ) \ge n - 1 , \tag{4}
 \f]
 \f$ L( d ) \f$ the edges of the longest path from \f$ d \f$.

 Indeed, a walk may go round a reachable cycle as many times as it needs,
 and so has any length; without a reachable cycle every walk from \f$ d \f$
 is a path, and its length is at most \f$ L( d ) \f$. `linearTiling()`
 decides (4) by one visit in depth of \f$ H \f$ from \f$ d \f$, which finds a
 reachable cycle as a back edge (see the theory of graphs) and computes
 \f$ L \f$ in the postvisit, in \f$ O( m^2 ) \f$ on the implicit matrix of
 \f$ H \f$, whatever \f$ n \f$ is; \f$ n \f$ enters the input with
 \f$ \log n \f$ bits, and an enumeration of the strips would take time
 exponential in it.

 The conclusion of the book pairs several NP-complete problems with a
 polynomial one that differs from it in a detail, and `EasyCases.h` realizes
 the polynomial halves that the other areas do not.

 <table>
 <caption>The polynomial halves of the pairs (table 19.1 of the
          book)</caption>
 <tr><th>NP-complete</th><th>Polynomial</th><th>Where</th></tr>
 <tr><td>SATISFIABILITY, three literals per clause</td>
     <td>two literals per clause</td><td><tt>twoSat()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>3-COLORABILITY</td><td>2-COLORABILITY</td>
     <td><tt>twoColoring()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>3-DIMENSIONAL MATCHING</td><td>2-DIMENSIONAL MATCHING</td>
     <td><tt>bipartiteMatching()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>CLIQUE</td><td>CLIQUE on an interval graph</td>
     <td><tt>intervalClique()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>BOUNDED TILING</td><td>the linear tiling</td>
     <td><tt>linearTiling()</tt> of <tt>Domino.h</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>HAMILTONIAN CIRCUIT</td><td>the Eulerian cycle</td>
     <td><tt>eulerianCycle()</tt> of <tt>EulerianCycle.h</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>KNAPSACK</td><td>the fractional knapsack</td>
     <td><tt>fractionalKnapsack()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>DEGREE-CONSTRAINED SPANNING TREE</td>
     <td>the minimum spanning tree</td><td><tt>kruskal()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>LONGEST PATH</td><td>the shortest path</td>
     <td><tt>shortestPaths()</tt></td></tr>
 </table>

 <em>Theorem 4 (Aspvall, Plass and Tarjan).</em> A formula of clauses of at
 most two literals is satisfiable if and only if no variable \f$ v \f$ lies
 in the strongly connected component of \f$ \neg v \f$ in the graph of the
 implications, which has the edges \f$ ( \neg a , b ) \f$ and
 \f$ ( \neg b , a ) \f$ for each clause \f$ a \vee b \f$.

 In fact, a path of implications from \f$ v \f$ to \f$ \neg v \f$ and one
 back would force \f$ v \f$ both true and false. Conversely, the assignment
 that makes \f$ v \f$ true when the component of \f$ v \f$ comes after that
 of \f$ \neg v \f$ in a topological order of the components, as
 `twoSat()` does, never sets a literal true with a false one among its
 consequences, since the graph is symmetric under negation; hence it
 satisfies each clause, in \f$ O( n + m ) \f$ by the algorithm of Kosaraju.
 Similarly, a graph is 2-colorable if and only if it has no odd cycle, which
 the visit in breadth of `twoColoring()` finds as an edge between two nodes of
 the same color; a bipartite matching is a maximum flow with unit capacities
 (see the theory of graphs); and on intervals of the line a set that meets
 pairwise has a common point (the property of Helly in one dimension, the
 latest left end lying before the earliest right end), so that the largest
 clique is the largest number of intervals through one point, which a sweep
 of the sorted ends counts in \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$.

